WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.708
Lesson 4 refines the energy-ladder picture by drawing a crucial boundary.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:11.415
Not every absorbed packet frees the electron.

3
00:00:11.415 --> 00:00:17.122
Some packets only raise the electron to a higher allowed bound level.

4
00:00:17.122 --> 00:00:22.830
That is excitation.

5
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:28.537
Ionisation is different.

6
00:00:28.537 --> 00:00:34.245
It occurs when enough energy is supplied to remove the electron from the atom entirely, placing it in the continuum beyond the ionisation threshold.

7
00:00:34.245 --> 00:00:39.952
This matters because later spectroscopy, photoelectric, and atomic-energy arguments become much clearer when the learner stops using excitation and ionisation as almost interchangeable words.

8
00:00:39.952 --> 00:00:45.660
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson becomes secure when the learner asks bound or free before using any threshold word.

9
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:51.367
Excitation means still bound.

10
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:57.075
The electron ends in a higher allowed state but remains attached to the atom.

11
00:00:57.075 --> 00:01:02.782
Ionisation means fully free.

12
00:01:02.782 --> 00:01:08.490
The electron leaves the atom and enters the continuum.

13
00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:15.309
This diagram matters because it places the key distinction in one picture.

14
00:01:15.309 --> 00:01:22.128
The bound ladder has several allowed levels.

15
00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:28.946
An excitation moves the electron upward within that ladder.

16
00:01:28.946 --> 00:01:35.765
Ionisation lies beyond the top threshold, where the electron is no longer bound to the atom.

17
00:01:35.765 --> 00:01:42.584
Once students can point to the continuum line and explain that crossing it changes the status of the electron completely, the language of thresholds becomes much more precise.

18
00:01:42.584 --> 00:01:49.402
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong threshold diagram makes the difference between higher bound and fully free impossible to blur.

19
00:01:49.402 --> 00:01:56.221
Bound ladder: These are the allowed atomic states that still belong to the atom.

20
00:01:56.221 --> 00:02:03.040
Excited bound state: A raised state above ground can still remain part of the ladder.

21
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:09.264
A helpful analogy is a person inside a building.

22
00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:15.488
Climbing from one balcony to a higher balcony is like excitation: the person is higher, but still inside the building.

23
00:02:15.488 --> 00:02:21.712
Leaving the building altogether is like ionisation: the person is no longer part of the same enclosed structure.

24
00:02:21.712 --> 00:02:27.936
The analogy matters because it makes the final-state difference feel obvious.

25
00:02:27.936 --> 00:02:34.160
The total energy may be larger in both cases, but the physical outcome is not the same kind of outcome.

26
00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:40.384
Keep this takeaway in view: The building analogy helps the learner distinguish between higher bound states and complete escape with much less confusion.

27
00:02:40.384 --> 00:02:46.608
Higher balcony means excited but inside.

28
00:02:46.608 --> 00:02:52.832
The system has more energy but remains within the same bound structure.

29
00:02:52.832 --> 00:02:59.056
Outside building means free electron.

30
00:02:59.056 --> 00:03:05.280
The electron has crossed the threshold into the continuum.

31
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.477
The core rules are that excitation keeps the electron bound, while ionisation removes it from the atom completely.

32
00:03:11.477 --> 00:03:17.673
A bound-state transition needs enough energy for a specific allowed gap.

33
00:03:17.673 --> 00:03:23.870
Ionisation needs enough energy to cross the top threshold into the continuum.

34
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:30.067
This is why ionisation is not just a larger ordinary excitation.

35
00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:36.263
The final state belongs to a different category.

36
00:03:36.263 --> 00:03:42.460
The learner who can say that clearly is already close to mastering the lesson.

37
00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:48.657
Keep this takeaway in view: The threshold rules become powerful when students classify outcomes by final state rather than by loose energy adjectives.

38
00:03:48.657 --> 00:03:54.853
If the electron is still bound: The result should be classified as excitation rather than ionisation.

39
00:03:54.853 --> 00:04:01.050
If the threshold is crossed: The learner should shift to ionisation language and a free-electron picture.

40
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:07.247
excitation = higher bound state.

41
00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:13.443
ionisation = full escape beyond the threshold.

42
00:04:13.443 --> 00:04:19.640
The crucial distinction is the final status of the electron, not just the word larger or smaller.

43
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:26.214
A useful routine is to ask whether the electron ends up bound to the atom or free from it.

44
00:04:26.214 --> 00:04:32.789
If the electron remains on the ladder, the outcome is excitation.

45
00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:39.363
If the electron ends up beyond the threshold in the continuum, the outcome is ionisation.

46
00:04:39.363 --> 00:04:45.938
This simple first question prevents many vocabulary mistakes and forces the learner to focus on the physical end state rather than on the size of the packet alone.

47
00:04:45.938 --> 00:04:52.512
Keep this takeaway in view: The bound-or-free routine turns a potentially fuzzy lesson into a crisp classification task.

48
00:04:52.512 --> 00:04:59.087
Step 1 means bound or free?.

49
00:04:59.087 --> 00:05:05.661
Let the electron's final status control the classification.

50
00:05:05.661 --> 00:05:12.236
Step 2 means locate threshold.

51
00:05:12.236 --> 00:05:18.810
Check whether the energy reached a bound gap or crossed the continuum boundary.

52
00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.743
Try four checks.

53
00:05:22.743 --> 00:05:26.675
If the electron ends in a higher allowed level but stays attached to the atom, is that excitation?

54
00:05:26.675 --> 00:05:30.608
Yes.

55
00:05:30.608 --> 00:05:34.541
If the electron leaves the atom completely, is that ionisation?

56
00:05:34.541 --> 00:05:38.473
Yes.

57
00:05:38.473 --> 00:05:42.406
Is the continuum above the ionisation threshold?

58
00:05:42.406 --> 00:05:46.339
Yes.

59
00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:50.271
And should every large absorbed packet automatically be described as ionising without checking the final state?

60
00:05:50.271 --> 00:05:54.204
No.

61
00:05:54.204 --> 00:05:58.137
Those are the exact distinctions the worked example needs.

62
00:05:58.137 --> 00:06:02.069
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can classify outcomes from the electron's final status instead of from vague energy language.

63
00:06:02.069 --> 00:06:06.002
Higher bound level means excitation.

64
00:06:06.002 --> 00:06:09.935
The electron remains part of the atom's allowed ladder.

65
00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:13.867
Full escape means ionisation.

66
00:06:13.867 --> 00:06:17.800
The electron crosses into the continuum and is no longer bound.

67
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:23.685
You are ready for the worked example now.

68
00:06:23.685 --> 00:06:29.571
Start by locating the electron's final state relative to the ladder and the continuum threshold.

69
00:06:29.571 --> 00:06:35.456
If it remains on the ladder, use excitation language.

70
00:06:35.456 --> 00:06:41.342
If it ends beyond the threshold, use ionisation language.

71
00:06:41.342 --> 00:06:47.227
The strongest answers in this lesson explain why the boundary matters before they state the final label.

72
00:06:47.227 --> 00:06:53.113
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the learner can now tell excitation and ionisation apart by checking the final quantum state.

73
00:06:53.113 --> 00:06:58.998
State move: Use the diagram to decide whether the electron remains bound or becomes free.

74
00:06:58.998 --> 00:07:04.884
Boundary move: Let the continuum threshold separate the two possible outcome types.

75
00:07:04.884 --> 00:07:10.769
identify the electron's final state relative to the ladder.

76
00:07:10.769 --> 00:07:16.655
check whether the ionisation threshold has been crossed.

77
00:07:16.655 --> 00:07:22.540
A strong solution uses the threshold boundary as the central reasoning step.
